{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260523231001524b25925b9e4cc1","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.1","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"There's no `msedge.exe` running, only `msedgewebview2.exe` processes. These are Edge WebView2 processes used by other applications (like the DevTools IDE itself), not the Edge browser. So we can't connect via CDP to a running Edge browser.\n\nLet me try yet another approach. Let me see if we can:\n1. Start Edge with remote debugging enabled and navigate to the developer console\n2. Or use Playwright to launch a browser and navigate through the login process\n\nBut for the login process, we'd need the user's credentials and possibly a verification code.\n\nActually, let me step back and think about what we CAN do:\n1. We have the AppID and AppSecret\n2. The AppSecret might be usable as a token for the open platform API\n3. The CLI tool `tma` accepted it as a token\n\nThe error we got from `tma upload` was about project type mismatch, not authentication failure. So the token actually works for authentication! The issue is that the AppID `tte7a1911c79c6fc8302` is registered as a mini-program (小程序) but our project is configured as a game.\n\nSo the real question is: can we change the project to work with a mini-program AppID?\n\nFor Douyin:\n- Mini-program (小程序): uses `app.js`, `app.json`, `compileType: \"miniapp\"` or similar\n- Mini-game (小游戏): uses `game.js`, `game.json`, `compileType: \"game\"`\n\nThe game uses HTML5 Canvas which is the game framework. But if the AppID is for a mini-program, we might need to adapt.\n\nActually wait, maybe the solution is simpler. The user registered a mini-program ID on the Douyin platform. Maybe they need to create a mini-game project instead. But we can't do that from here.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can modify the project to be a mini-program that contains the game. Mini-programs can have web-view components that load HTML content, or they can use Canvas within the mini-program framework.\n\nBut that would be a significant rewrite. Let me try a different approach:\n1. Change the project config to match what the AppID expects\n2. See if we can upload it as a mini-program with the game logic embedded\n\nActually, let me first try changing `compileType` from `\"game\"` to something else and see what the CLI expects.\n\nWait, looking at the error message again: \"当前项目为小玩法项目，但是 'tte7a1911c79c6fc8302' 属于小程序 ID\"\n\n\"小玩法\" - this is a specific category on Douyin, literally \"mini gameplay\". The project is configured as a 小玩法 but the AppID is for 小程序.\n\nFor Douyin:\n- 小程序 = mini-program\n- 小游戏 = mini-game (the one with game.js/game.json)\n- 小玩法 = a newer category, maybe for interactive mini-games within the video feed\n\nThe `compileType: \"game\"` might map to 小玩法 in the newer CLI versions.\n\nLet me check what compileType values are available and what they map to. Maybe I need to change it to something specific.\n\nActually, let me just try changing the compileType to different values and see if any of them work:\n- \"miniapp\" - for mini-programs\n- \"mini-program\" - maybe\n- \"game\" - current, doesn't match\n\nOr maybe the issue is something else entirely. Let me look at the project.config.json more carefully.\n\nActually, the simplest fix might be to change the project structure to be a mini-program instead of a game. A mini-program can still contain Canvas-based games. Let me:\n1. Rename game.js to app.js\n2. Create app.json from game.json\n3. Change compileType to the correct value\n4. Try uploading again\n\nBut wait - the game.js exports a different API than what app.js expects. In a mini-program, app.js exports App({}), while in a game, game.js exports... well, it depends on the framework.\n\nLet me look at the game.js to understand its structure.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"33ad